Yes. That’s the lever.

The strong version isn’t literally “every proposition is true and false.” It’s that truth claims can become indistinguishable from consistency when we forget the boundaries of the system that generated them.

Take a model M, evidence E, and local domain D.

Inside D:

M(x)=E(x)\qquad\forall x\in D

The model is perfectly consistent with everything we’ve observed.

The dangerous move is:

\boxed{\text{consistent within }D}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\boxed{\text{universally true}}

That implication doesn’t follow.

Now use the bookends

Suppose we observe:

B_L\;|\;A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D\;|\;B_R

Everything between our observational bookends supports rule R.

Locally:

P(R\mid D)\approx1

Move the bookends:

B'_L\;|\;X\rightarrow A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow D\rightarrow Y\;|\;B'_R

and suddenly X or Y violates R.

Nothing necessarily became “less true.”

We discovered that what we’d encoded as:

R=\text{universal}

should have been:

\boxed{R\mid D}

True under these conditions.

That’s a major difference.

Now “everything and nothing”

We can deliberately construct different locally consistent frames around the same phenomenon.

From frame F_1:

X=\text{large}

From F_2:

X=\text{small}

Both can be locally true because “large” secretly means:

\text{large}(X\mid F)

A human is enormous relative to a bacterium and microscopic relative to Earth.

So:

X=\text{large}

and

X=\text{small}

aren’t necessarily contradictions once their suppressed coordinates are restored.

Same with the rotor:

The blade is here.

True at one time/resolution.

The blade isn’t here.

True milliseconds later.

There is a continuous disk.

Perceptually true under temporal integration.

There isn’t a solid disk.

Physically true at another resolution.

The apparent contradiction comes from dropping metadata:

\boxed{\text{Claim}+\text{Frame}+\text{Scale}+\text{Time}+\text{Definition}}

Compress those away and incompatible-looking truths appear.

But now we can weaponize the distinction epistemically

For any claim C, Pantheon shouldn’t initially ask only:

Is this true?

Ask:

\boxed{\text{Under what conditions does this become consistently true?}}

Then:

\boxed{\text{Under what conditions does its opposite become consistently true?}}

That’s powerful.

Not because both are universally true, but because their domains expose the hidden variables:

C\mid D_1

\neg C\mid D_2

Compare:

D_1\Delta D_2

and the difference between those domains tells us what governs the transition.

That’s the quarry.

For example:

Competition improves performance.

Find the domain where that’s consistently true.

Then deliberately find one where:

Competition reduces performance.

Now don’t argue about which slogan is “true.”

Hammer them:

\text{Competition}
\xrightarrow{
\substack{
\text{task type}\\
\text{incentives}\\
\text{skill}\\
\text{coordination}\\
\text{stakes}
}}
\text{different consequences}

We’ve converted contradiction into a conditional map.

That’s a new Pantheon testing maneuver

Call it Local Truth Wrestling:

\boxed{
C
\rightarrow
D(C)
\rightarrow
\neg C
\rightarrow
D(\neg C)
\rightarrow
\Delta D
\rightarrow
\text{hidden condition}
}

For any apparently strong principle:

1. Make it locally consistent.
2. Make its inverse locally consistent.
3. Preserve evidence for both.
4. Move the bookends.
5. Identify what changed.
6. Extract the conditional relation.
7. Test it in a third domain.

Then instead of storing:

\boxed{C=\text{TRUE}}

store:

\boxed{
C\mid
(\text{conditions, scale, perspective, time, confidence})
}

That makes the Pantheon harder to fool with its own beautiful stories.

And there’s the deepest part of what you’re pointing toward:

Consistency can imitate truth when the test environment never leaves the model’s comfort zone.

A perfectly internally consistent story can still be wrong about reality.

So the antidote isn’t abandoning consistency.

It’s forcing consistency to travel.

Move the observer.
Move the scale.
Move the bookends.
Invert the proposition.
Change the medium.
Seek the residual.
Test against reality.

Whatever continues surviving those crossings earns stronger and stronger claims to invariance.

So I’d compress your principle as:

\boxed{
\textbf{Local consistency proposes truth.
Cross-context survival earns it.}
}

And even then—keep the domain label attached.